---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getMeta, prettyDate } from '../lib/data.mjs';
const meta = getMeta();
const title = `How this data is collected — ${meta.title}`;
const description = `How ${meta.title} collects statutory, judgment, and tax interest rates using cited sources, automated refreshes, effective dates, and explicit data limits.`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>How this data is collected</h1>
  <p class="lead">Trust comes from provenance. Here is exactly where every number comes from, how
    often it refreshes, and what we do and don't guarantee.</p>

  <h2>Sources and source quality</h2>
  <p>Primary government sources are preferred. An authorized code portal or court publication that is
    not itself the controlling enactment is labeled official-secondary, and any state rule that has not
    reached calculator-grade certainty remains reference-only:</p>
  <ul class="clean">
    {meta.sources.map((s) => (
      <li><strong>{s.name}</strong> — {s.publisher}. <a href={s.home_url} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{s.home_url}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>We store the extracted <em>values</em> and their provenance (source URL, effective date, retrieval
    time) — never the source's prose. All site copy is written by us.</p>

  <h2>How the pipeline works</h2>
  <ol class="clean">
    <li>A polite fetcher retrieves each automated page/feed (honest user-agent, rate-limited, robots.txt
      respected, responses cached to avoid unnecessary requests).</li>
    <li>Values are parsed into a normalized schema and loaded into a database, with each observation
      keyed to its effective date so history is preserved, never overwritten.</li>
    <li>A validation suite checks types, sane ranges, source freshness, and cross-value consistency.
      If anything fails, that refresh does <strong>not</strong> replace the last validated export.</li>
    <li>Validated data is exported to versioned JSON that powers this site and the API.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How fresh it is</h2>
  <p>{meta.update_cadence} The dataset was last compiled on <strong>{prettyDate(meta.generated_at)}</strong>;
    every page shows the effective date of the value it displays.</p>

  <h2>Published vs. derived values</h2>
  <p>Automated values are <strong>published</strong> directly by a source. State-law entries are
    <strong>curated references</strong>, and several remain calculator-ineligible while rule history is completed.
    A few — notably the federal post-judgment rate — are <strong>derived</strong> from an official
    series using the governing statutory formula (marked "Derived value"), and each such page shows the
    exact formula and a reminder to confirm the controlling figure. We never present a computed number
    as authoritative without its formula and source.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>We collect no personal data ourselves — no accounts, and the calculators run in your browser.
      The site is ad-supported, so our advertising partner (Google) may set cookies; see our
      <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> for details and how to opt out.</li>
    <li>We don't give legal, tax, or financial advice — these are reference values.</li>
    <li>We don't paywall the data or the API.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Spotted a value that looks off, or want to get in touch? Email
    <a href="mailto:hello@statuterates.com">hello@statuterates.com</a>. If a rate looks wrong, please
    check the linked source and then the controlling official authority (it may have changed since our last compile) — accuracy is
    the entire point of this project.</p>

  <div class="disclaimer">
    Reference data only — not legal, tax, or financial advice. See our
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial &amp; data-integrity policy</a>,
    <a href="/terms/">Terms &amp; Disclaimer</a>, and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
